newda='»Og endelig: Jeg springer ned for at se, om vidnet mod mig lever, og samtidig efterlader jeg et andet vidne på stien, nemlig den støder, jeg tog hos de to kvinder. De vil begge altid kunne genkende den som deres og bevidne, at det var mig, der tog den. Og jeg glemte den ikke bare på stien eller tabte den i distraktion og forvirring: nej, vi kastede ligefrem vores våben fra os, for det blev fundet femten skridt fra det sted, hvor Grigorij faldt. Hvorfor gjorde vi det? Netop fordi det gjorde os ondt, at vi havde dræbt et menneske, en gammel tjener. Derfor kastede vi i fortvivlelse og med en forbandelse støderen fra os som et mordvåben. Anderledes kan det ikke være: hvorfor ellers kaste den med sådan en kraft? Og hvis vi kunne føle smerte og medlidenhed over at have dræbt et menneske, var det naturligvis, fordi vi ikke havde dræbt vores far. Havde vi dræbt ham, ville vi ikke være sprunget ned til det andet offer af medlidenhed. Så ville følelsen have været en anden; vi ville have tænkt på at redde os selv, ikke på medlidenhed. Sådan er det. Tværtimod, gentager jeg, ville vi have knust hans kranium helt i stedet for at pusle om ham i fem minutter. Der blev plads til medlidenhed og en god følelse, netop fordi samvittigheden indtil da var ren. Her har vi altså en anden psykologi. Jeg har netop selv grebet til psykologien med vilje, mine herrer i juryen, for at vise anskueligt, at man kan udlede hvad som helst af den. Alt afhænger af, hvem der bruger den. Psykologien lokker selv de mest alvorlige mennesker til at skrive romaner, helt uden at de vil det. Jeg taler om overdreven psykologi, mine herrer i juryen, om en vis form for misbrug af den.«'
newen='“And finally, I jump down to see whether the witness against me is alive, and at the same time I leave another witness on the path: the very pestle I took from the two women, both of whom can always recognize it as theirs and testify that I took it. Nor did I simply forget it on the path or drop it in distraction and confusion. No, we deliberately threw away our weapon, for it was found fifteen paces from where Grigory fell. Why did we do that? Precisely because we felt bitterly that we had killed a man, an old servant, and so, in distress and with a curse, we threw away the pestle as a murder weapon. It cannot have been otherwise: why else fling it so far? And if we could feel grief and pity at having killed a man, that was surely because we had not killed our father. Had we killed him, we would not have jumped down to the other victim out of pity. We would have felt differently then; we would have been thinking of saving ourselves, not of pity. That is certain. On the contrary, I repeat, we would have smashed his skull completely instead of tending him for five minutes. There was room for pity and a good feeling precisely because the conscience had been clear until then. Here, then, is a different psychology. I have deliberately resorted to psychology myself, gentlemen of the jury, to show plainly that anything at all can be deduced from it. Everything depends on whose hands it is in. Psychology tempts even the most serious people into writing novels, quite involuntarily. I am speaking of excessive psychology, gentlemen of the jury, of a certain abuse of it.”'
